    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="text-sm text-white/60">*Revolut charges 0.5-1% markup on weekends and above monthly limits</p>

  <!-- Fees & Exchange Rates -->
  <h2 id="fees">Fees and Exchange Rates Explained</h2>

  <h3>The Mid-Market Rate</h3>
  <p>
    The "mid-market rate" (or interbank rate) is the real exchange rate—the midpoint between
    buy and sell rates that banks use when trading with each other. It's what Google shows
    when you search "USD to EUR."
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Traditional banks don't give you this rate.</strong> They add 2-4% markup, hidden
    in the rate itself. A bank might show "no fees" while giving you a rate that's 3% worse
    than mid-market.
  </p>

  <h3>How Wise Charges</h3>
  <p>
    Wise shows the mid-market rate and adds a transparent percentage fee:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>USD → EUR: typically 0.41%</li>
    <li>GBP → EUR: typically 0.35%</li>
    <li>USD → GBP: typically 0.46%</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You see exactly what you're paying before confirming.
  </p>

  <h3>Revolut's Model</h3>
  <p>
    Revolut offers the interbank rate with limits:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Weekdays:</strong> Interbank rate up to monthly limit (varies by plan)</li>
    <li><strong>Weekends:</strong> 0.5-1% markup (markets are closed)</li>
    <li><strong>Over limit:</strong> 0.5-1% fee applies</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The Bottom Line</h3>
  <p>
    For a $5,000 conversion:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Traditional bank:</strong> ~$150-200 lost (3-4% markup)</li>
    <li><strong>Wise:</strong> ~$25 (0.5% fee)</li>
    <li><strong>Revolut (weekday, under limit):</strong> ~$0 fee</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Debit Cards -->
  <h2 id="debit-cards">Debit Card Comparison</h2>

  <h3>Wise Card</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Spend in any currency at the mid-market rate + small fee</li>
    <li>Free ATM withdrawals up to £200/month, then 1.75%</li>
    <li>Works anywhere Mastercard is accepted</li>
    <li>Freeze/unfreeze instantly in app</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Revolut Card</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Spend at interbank rate (weekday limits apply)</li>
    <li>Free ATM withdrawals vary by plan (£200-unlimited)</li>
    <li>Virtual cards for online shopping</li>
    <li>Disposable virtual cards for security</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Payoneer Card</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>$29.95 annual fee</li>
    <li>ATM withdrawal: $3.15 per transaction</li>
    <li>Best for withdrawing Payoneer balance locally</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Tips for Card Use</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Always pay in local currency:</strong> Decline "conversion" at terminals</li>
    <li><strong>Notify of travel:</strong> Some cards need travel notifications</li>
    <li><strong>Carry backup:</strong> Have at least two cards from different providers</li>
    <li><strong>Withdraw wisely:</strong> Fewer, larger ATM withdrawals minimize fees</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Country Availability -->
  <h2 id="availability">Country Availability</h2>

  <h3>Wise</h3>
  <p>
    Available to residents of 160+ countries. US, UK, EU, Australia, and most of Asia/Africa supported.
    Some features vary by location.
  </p>

  <h3>Revolut</h3>
  <p>
    Strong in UK and Europe. US availability limited (basic accounts only). Not available in many
    Asian and African countries.
  </p>

  <h3>N26</h3>
  <p>
    EU and EEA only. Not available in US (withdrew from market in 2022). Limited global reach.
  </p>

  <h3>Payoneer</h3>
  <p>
    Available in 200+ countries. Strong in markets where other services are limited (India, Pakistan,
    Bangladesh, etc.). Good option for developing markets.
  </p>

  <!-- Setting Up Your System -->
  <h2 id="system-setup">Setting Up Your Multi-Currency System</h2>

  <h3>The Recommended Stack</h3>
  <p>
    Most digital nomads benefit from multiple accounts:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Home country bank:</strong> Keep for tax payments, credit history, emergencies</li>
    <li><strong>Wise:</strong> Primary for receiving client payments in multiple currencies</li>
    <li><strong>Revolut or N26:</strong> Daily spending and travel</li>
    <li><strong>Payoneer:</strong> If you use platforms that integrate with it</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Receiving Payments</h3>
  <p>
    Set up local bank details in currencies you frequently receive:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>US clients:</strong> Give them your US account details (ACH transfers)</li>
    <li><strong>UK clients:</strong> Give them UK sort code and account number</li>
    <li><strong>EU clients:</strong> Give them SEPA-compatible IBAN</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Clients pay local transfers (fast, free for them), money arrives in that currency.
  </p>

  <h3>Converting Currency</h3>
  <p>
    Don't convert automatically. Instead:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Hold balances in currencies you'll spend</li>
    <li>Convert when rates are favorable (Wise shows historical charts)</li>
    <li>Batch conversions to minimize fees on small amounts</li>
    <li>Avoid weekend conversions on Revolut</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Spending Strategy</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Use the card linked to your balance in the local currency</li>
    <li>When traveling in eurozone with EUR balance—spend from EUR</li>
    <li>When you don't have local currency, the card auto-converts at good rates</li>
    <li>Track spending by category in the app</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <h2>Conclusion: Bank Like a Global Citizen</h2>
  <p>
    Traditional banking wasn't built for people who earn in one currency, spend in another, and
    move between countries. Multi-currency accounts are.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Your action plan:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Open a Wise account for receiving payments (takes 10 minutes)</li>
    <li>Order the free debit card</li>
    <li>Set up local bank details in currencies you receive</li>
    <li>Consider Revolut for daily spending if you're in Europe</li>
    <li>Keep your home country account for backup and local obligations</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Stop losing thousands of dollars a year to hidden bank fees. Your money should work as
    globally as you do.
  </p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
